1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,016
How does the bank know a payment request really came from the shop, and only once?

2
00:00:05,066 --> 00:00:17,520
First, a setup chain: the merchant signs up by email code, two admins approve it, its sales point goes live, it gets an e POS terminal, a commission rule, an API client, and a signing key in Vault.

3
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:24,600
The shop's server asks for a payment: a website, an order number, an amount in tiyin, and a return address.

4
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:29,496
Its access token comes from its API client, which carries the merchant role.

5
00:00:29,546 --> 00:00:36,000
But a stolen token alone must not be enough to create payments, so every request is also signed.

6
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:51,000
It follows R F C ninety-four twenty-one, HTTP Message Signatures: the server signs the method, the full address, its token, the body's digest, a creation time and a nonce, with HMAC SHA two fifty six.

7
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:59,376
Each API client has its own signing key, which the bank puts straight into Vault; our app never generates it, and only reads it.

8
00:00:59,426 --> 00:01:07,704
A key read from Vault is kept only for the configured time, and an unreachable Vault refuses the request instead of falling back to an old key.

9
00:01:07,754 --> 00:01:15,216
A signature created outside the configured window is refused, and a nonce works only once, remembered in Redis.

10
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:21,648
The signature is checked before the nonce is spent, so a forged request can't burn the merchant's nonces.

11
00:01:21,698 --> 00:01:28,248
A network retry must never create a second payment, so each request carries an idempotency key.

12
00:01:28,298 --> 00:01:34,488
The same key with the same body returns the stored response, and the same key with a different body is refused.

13
00:01:34,538 --> 00:01:41,520
Two retries at the same moment meet at the database's unique keys, and the loser replays the winner's answer.

14
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:49,464
Only the merchant's own live website, with one of its return addresses, can take a payment, and anything else is refused before saving.

15
00:01:49,514 --> 00:02:00,936
The response carries a one-time link to the bank's payment page, and the database keeps only its hash; the payment's other columns come in episodes fifteen, sixteen, twenty and twenty-one.

16
00:02:00,986 --> 00:02:04,584
Next, the buyer opens that page and enters their card.
